storage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_15220.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>102. Shri Kesariya Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: A black-colored image of Lord Shri Kesariya Parshwanath, seated in the half-lotus (ardha-padmasana) posture, approximately 152 centimeters high, with a canopy of seven serpent hoods above its head. This image is believed to resemble the image of Antariksha Parshwanath.</p><p>Location: This temple stands in a vast garden in the area around the village of Bhadravati.</p><p>History: The remains found at this pilgrimage site indicate that it is very ancient — though exactly how old remains unknown. There is a legend behind how this image was discovered. In Vikram Samvat 1966, the trustees of &quot;Antariksha Parshwanath Tirth&quot; had a dream in which they saw a serpent-deity (nagdevata) standing behind them in a forest, fearlessly. Then a miracle occurred: within this vision they saw an ancient temple containing an image of Kesariya Parshwanath. The serpent-deity told them to renovate the ancient temple and install the image there. A search for the temple began, and this image was found in the ruins of a temple buried in the ground. The villagers called it &quot;Kesariya Baba&quot; and applied vermillion (sindoor) to it. Several Jain monks together took the image under their protection. In 1912, this pilgrimage site was handed over by the government to the Shwetambar Jain Sangh. 10.5 acres of land were donated to the sangh for the temple. The sangh then had the temple renovated and installed the image of Kesariya Parshwanath in Vikram Samvat 1976. Since the image&#x27;s existence became known through a dream, it is also called &quot;Swapnadev Kesariya Parshwanath.&quot;</p><p>Many miracles continue to occur here even today. A fair is held here every year on the tenth day of the dark half of the month of Paush. Under the guidance of Acharya Vikramsurishwarji Maharaj Saheb, a six-month tripadi (three-part) religious procession was traveling from Secunderabad in Andhra to Shikharji in Bengal. At that time, the India-Pakistan war was underway, and everyone was fearful of what might happen. The Acharya composed a hymn (stavan) to Kesariya Parshwanath, and the entire sangh began singing it daily. A miracle occurred — the war ended within 19 days. The sangh completed its journey successfully.</p><p>Other Temples: There is also a temple of Lord Adinath and a Guru Mandir. These temples are within the garden.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: Many images found here were recovered from the ground. Remains from ruined structures are also artistically delightful. The architectural remains in and around the village are of great antiquity and interest. The Archaeological Survey of India has declared this pilgrimage site a protected monument.</p><p>Directions: The nearest railway station is Bhandak, one and a half kilometers away, while Chandrapur (Chanda) railway station is 32 kilometers from this pilgrimage site. Bus service and private vehicles are available. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available, along with an upashraya. Since all these facilities are set within a beautiful garden, the pilgrimage site is all the more attractive.</p><p>Scripture References: This temple is mentioned in the &quot;365 Shri Parshwa Jin Nammala.&quot; The renowned Acharya Shri Bhadrabahuswami traveled through this city along with Chandragupta Maurya, and practiced yoga-sadhana in its caves. The Chinese traveler Hiuen Tsang mentioned this city in his writings, praising its king as devout and knowledgeable in the arts. This city was a center of learning for Jain, Buddhist, and Vedic literature. The princess of Bhadravati was queen to Kharavela, emperor of Kalinga. An image of Kesariya Parshwanath is also found in the temple at Dima, in Banaskantha, Gujarat.</p><p>Praise (Stuti): He who appears through dreams and cuts through all troubles; though seemingly detached, he still captivates the mind; radiant in Bhadravati, showering auspiciousness all around. With devotion I bow to Lord Shri Kesariya Parshwa.</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Uvasaggaharam Parshwanathaya Namah.&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri 108 Swapnadev Kesariya Parshwanath Jain Shwetambar Mandir, Bhadravati - 442902, Post: Bhadravati, District: Chandrapur, State: Maharashtra, India. Phone: 07175-66030, 07875031440.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=