government to the Shwetambar Jain Sangh. 10.5 acres of land were donated to the sangh for the temple. The sangh then had the temple renovated and installed the image of Kesariya Parshwanath in Vikram Samvat 1976. Since the image's existence became known through a dream, it is also called \\\"Swapnadev Kesariya Parshwanath.\\\"\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"102. Shri Kesariya Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: A black-colored image of Lord Shri Kesariya Parshwanath, seated in the half-lotus (ardha-padmasana) posture, approximately 152 centimeters high, with a canopy of seven serpent hoods above its head. This image is believed to resemble the image of Antariksha Parshwanath.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: This temple stands in a vast garden in the area around the village of Bhadravati.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Many miracles continue to occur here even today. A fair is held here every year on the tenth day of the dark half of the month of Paush. Under the guidance of Acharya Vikramsurishwarji Maharaj Saheb, a six-month tripadi (three-part) religious procession was traveling from Secunderabad in Andhra to Shikharji in Bengal. At that time, the India-Pakistan war was underway, and everyone was fearful of what might happen. The Acharya composed a hymn (stavan) to Kesariya Parshwanath, and the entire sangh began singing it daily. A miracle occurred — the war ended within 19 days. The sangh completed its journey successfully.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: There is also a temple of Lord Adinath and a Guru Mandir. These temples are within the garden.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Art and Sculpture: Many images found here were recovered from the ground. Remains from ruined structures are also artistically delightful. The architectural remains in and around the village are of great antiquity and interest. The Archaeological Survey of India has declared this pilgrimage site a protected monument.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: The nearest railway station is Bhandak, one and a half kilometers away, while Chandrapur (Chanda) railway station is 32 kilometers from this pilgrimage site. Bus service and private vehicles are available. Dharmashala and bhojanshala facilities are available, along with an upashraya. Since all these facilities are set within a beautiful garden, the pilgrimage site is all the more attractive.\\\"}],\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-124\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":[[\\\"path\\\",{\\\"d\\\":\\\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\\\",\\\"key\\\":\\\"1wnfg3\\\"}]],\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\\\",\\\"children\\\":\\\"Shri Uvsaggaharam Parshwanath\\\"}]]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L5\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-126\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"span\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"m